; Block npm lifecycle scripts (preinstall / install / postinstall) repo-wide.
;
; No bus tool call is ever gated on a human: production turns run
; permissionMode 'default' with a canUseTool callback that auto-approves
; everything except AskUserQuestion. (Both routers wire the ask-gate hook,
; so the bypassPermissions branch in runner.ts is reached only by callers
; that skip it — i.e. tests. See the note in server/src/bus/guardrail.ts.)
; Auto-approve-everything is bypass in effect, so a malicious transitive
; postinstall script still lands as direct RCE on an operator's machine.
; The legitimate native build (better-sqlite3) is re-enabled explicitly via
; `npm rebuild better-sqlite3 --foreground-scripts` in CI and dev setup.
ignore-scripts=true

; Supply-chain hardening: refuse to resolve any version published < 7 days ago.
; A freshly-published (possibly account-takeover / malicious) release must age
; a week before npm will install it — the window in which most poisoned
; publishes are caught and yanked. `npm ci` installs the lockfile verbatim, so
; this only gates fresh *resolution* (`npm install`, transitive bumps); it pairs
; with the dependabot `cooldown` in .github/dependabot.yml.
min-release-age=7

; Turn an unsupported-Node install into a REFUSAL, not a warning.
;
; MEASURED, three ways, because the default is the opposite of what it looks
; like. Without this, a dependency whose `engines.node` excludes the running
; Node installs anyway: npm prints `npm warn EBADENGINE` and exits 0. With it,
; npm exits 1 and names the package, the required range and the actual version.
; It binds on DEPENDENCIES, not only on this package's own `engines`.
;
; WHY IT IS WORTH THE STRICTNESS. Two Dependabot majors (jsdom 30,
; better-sqlite3 13) both dropped Node 20 in their own `engines`, CI was still
; on Node 20, and npm installed them regardless. The failure then surfaced 80
; seconds later as 230 runtime `TypeError`s and a wall of dead vitest workers —
; a symptom that reads like a code defect and cost a full investigation to
; trace back to the runtime. The information was present at install time and
; was printed as a warning nobody reads.
;
; COST, MEASURED against the current tree: 233 packages declare `engines.node`;
; on Node 24 and 26 exactly ZERO of them would block an install. On Node 20 one
; would — `lint-staged`, which already requires >=22.22.1 and has been silently
; unsupported on CI. So this costs nothing today and only ever fires on the
; case it exists for.
engine-strict=true
